+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers edit the same plot visit on separate tablets, and some of those tablets can be offline for up to 48 hours. An edit is made against the visit state the tablet last fetched, so it can arrive after other edits have already been accepted. The record store must decide what a current visit is when such edits arrive, without treating arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+The choice matters because the tablet and review-export nodes take their shape from it. Submission metadata, the review work a coordinator sees, and the record states that may enter the Friday partner CSV all follow from this policy. Choosing the wrong policy would mean redesigning both nodes, not just this one.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates.** None. Both options keep every original submission (REC-004) and neither discards a submitted edit, so neither is eliminated by a gate.
+
+**Weighted criteria.**
+
+- **Coordinator review workload** — *major*. Derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Two coordinators review roughly 6,000 visit records per season.
+- **Whole-visit preservation** — *major*. Derives from the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*, read at the level of a worker's intent. A worker's edits to one visit, taken together, are the unit the worker reviewed in the field.
+- **Scientific relationship safety** — *major*. Derives from the non-goal *inference of scientific intent*. Combining edits field by field can produce a visit whose species identification and cover estimate come from different workers, and the server cannot see that the two fields belong together.
+- **Traceability to export** — *major*. Derives from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. A coordinator must be able to say which submission produced each exported value.
+- **Export coverage before Friday** — *moderate*. Derives from the goal *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*. A policy that holds more visits out of the export is harder to operate, even when it is safer.
+- **Merge rule maintenance** — *moderate*. The number and complexity of rules the team must maintain for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries.
+- **Submission and storage size** — *minor*. Whole revisions are larger than structured field changes, but visit records are small.
+
+**Latent criteria probed.** A critic would weigh *how a wrong automatic combination is discovered*. Under a merge policy, an incorrect combination is already in the current record and surfaces only if someone reviews it later. Under whole-visit revisions, nothing is combined without a coordinator's decision, so an error is visible at the point it could occur. This criterion survives and is folded into *Scientific relationship safety*.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it started from. The server stores each accepted submission as a complete visit revision. A visit has one current revision when its submissions form a single chain. When two submissions name the same base revision, or when a submission's base is not the current head, the visit has competing revisions. Each competing revision is a complete visit as one worker submitted it, and it stays unresolved until a coordinator resolves it. Resolution selects one revision or records a resolution revision that names its contributing submission IDs (REC-006). No revision is merged field by field.
+
+*Demonstration.* Worker A corrects an observer name and changes the cover estimate for *Ammophila arenaria*. Worker B, from the same starting state, changes only the cover estimate for the same species. The visit now has two competing revisions. A coordinator picks one, or records a resolution that keeps A's observer correction and B's cover estimate, with both submissions named as contributors.
+
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak** fit. Even edits to unrelated fields produce a review item. Every concurrent pair on a visit needs a decision, including pairs whose changes do not overlap.
+- Whole-visit preservation: **strong** fit. Each submitted visit remains intact as one revision.
+- Scientific relationship safety: **strong** fit. No field is combined without a coordinator seeing the whole revision it came from.
+- Traceability to export: **strong** fit. Each exported visit is one revision or one resolution with named contributors.
+- Export coverage before Friday: **weak** fit. Unresolved visits are excluded from the CSV until resolved.
+- Merge rule maintenance: **strong** fit. No field-level merge rules exist to maintain.
+- Submission and storage size: **partial** fit. Each submission carries a full visit revision.
+
+Summary: safest for the record, with the cost paid in coordinator attention.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries the base state it was made against and the field-level changes it makes. When the base is the current head, the server applies the changes. When the base is older, the server computes a three-way merge against the current state. Disjoint field changes combine automatically. Overlapping changes to the same field produce a review item. The merge rules are maintained per field type: scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries.
+
+*Demonstration.* The same two edits as above. A's observer correction touches a field B did not change, so it combines automatically. A's cover change for *Ammophila arenaria* and B's cover change for the same species touch the same field, so the visit goes to review. Had B instead changed the cover of a different species, the species entry rules would decide whether the two entries overlap.
+
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** fit. Only overlapping field changes reach review.
+- Whole-visit preservation: **weak** fit. A worker's submitted visit is decomposed into fields and recombined with other workers' fields, so no single stored revision matches what a worker submitted.
+- Scientific relationship safety: **weak** fit. Disjoint fields can still be scientifically linked, for example an identification and the cover estimate that depends on it. The rules cannot know all such links, so some combinations will be wrong.
+- Traceability to export: **partial** fit. Field-level provenance can be stored, but a coordinator must read provenance for each field to trace an exported value.
+- Export coverage before Friday: **strong** fit. More visits are combined and ready for export without review.
+- Merge rule maintenance: **weak** fit. Each field type needs maintained rules, and a new scientific relationship may need a new rule.
+- Submission and storage size: **strong** fit. Submissions carry base snapshots and structured changes rather than whole visits.
+
+Summary: lower review volume, paid for with maintained rules and combinations that no one has seen as a whole.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The choice turns on three tenets and the non-goal on scientific inference. Whole-visit revisions fit *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*, *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and the non-goal, all three of which are major. The merge policy would fit *Make reviewable problems visible* and reduce the export delay, but it is weaker on the major criteria and depends on rules the team would have to keep correct.
+
+This decision is taken against one tenet. *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* is weakened, because whole-visit revisions create a review item for concurrent edits that a merge policy would have combined automatically. The team has accepted that cost. Coordinators will see more review work per season, and this is the price of keeping whole worker submissions intact.
+
+**What the choice forecloses.** Automatic combination of disjoint field edits. A future move to merge would need a new policy, a migration of stored revisions, and a rule set for scalar, observer, and species fields.
+
+**What it obligates downstream.**
+
+- *Tablet-sync* keeps the base revision ID with each local submission. It shows a worker when a visit it edited now has competing revisions, and it keeps that worker's draft intact while the coordinator decides. It does not merge fields locally.
+- *Review-export* needs a revision comparison view that shows competing revisions side by side. It excludes unresolved visits from the Friday CSV and reports them as blocking review work. A resolution revision must carry the contributing submission IDs into the export's provenance.
+- Both nodes link back to the record-store contract rather than defining their own concurrency semantics. The contract, including submission envelope and resolution operation, is written in the record-store LLD in the next phase.
+
+**When to reconsider.** Reopen this decision if coordinator review time becomes the limiting factor for the Friday export, and most of the review items that reach a coordinator involve competing revisions whose changed fields do not overlap. The team should measure this from the first season's review queue. Reopening the decision would mean choosing a merge policy with rules for each field type, and treating the scientific relationship risk as a design constraint rather than a reason to avoid merging.
